{"claim_id":"RQ1-A","finding":"R0001 partitions an L-layer DNN across three heterogeneous infrastructure tiers (UAVs, ground edge servers, and a cloud server) using two layer cut points (p_k, q_k) that yield five execution situations, whereas R0014 executes autonomously across airborne UAV clusters without a ground station using two distinct levels of parallelism: inter-cluster pipeline partitioning into three sequential submodels and intra-cluster spatial input-data partitioning coordinated by cluster heads.","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0001","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/p08_extraction_table.json","key":"architecture_and_partition","locator":"physical p. 2, §II.A; physical p. 3, §III.A","support":"Three-tier UAV-edge-cloud architecture evaluated with "This experiment considers 10 UAVs, 4 ESs and one cloud server." and two partition points (p_k, q_k) defining "There are five situations: 1. The inference service only executes on UAV." (UAV-only, UAV+ES, UAV+ES+cloud, ES-only, ES+cloud)."},{"report_id":"R0014","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_01_accepted.json","key":"partition_assignment","locator":"physical p. 4, §III, Fig. 3; physical p. 6, §IV.B","support":"Airborne-only multi-cluster execution without a ground station where "the complete DL model is partitioned into three submodels for each cluster" and "results of submodels flow in a pipeline parallel manner" (ECLB)."},{"report_id":"R0014","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","key":"intra_cluster_spatial_partition","locator":"physical p. 5, §III; physical pp. 5-6, §IV.A, Algorithms 1-2","support":"Within each cluster, "the data is split into several segments and distributed to UAVs in the cluster by the cluster head UAVs and computed in parallel" via spatial input ranges (MRT and ICLB)."}],"quantitative":[]}
{"claim_id":"RQ1-B","finding":"Keeping the assignment unit separate from network topology, R0092 uses a decentralized peer-to-peer UAV swarm without a central coordinator to assign a two-part sequential layer partition (cut layer l_cut, Part A on source UAV u_i and Part B on one chosen peer u_k from Pareto Top-K=3, pipelined over mini-batches with local fallback), whereas R0118 routes each detected target as a whole raw payload to a single self-contained specialized MoE expert hosted per UAV (selected by a learnable router on the Center UAV) over a multi-hop FANET relay mesh maintained by a centralized trajectory planner (with a decentralized MARL variant also evaluated).","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0092","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_02_accepted.json","key":"partition_assignment","locator":"physical p. 5, §III.E; physical p. 6, §III.G, §IV.A; physical p. 7, §IV.B.2","support":"Per-task two-UAV sequential layer split at l_cut (Part A layers 1..l_cut on source UAV u_i, Part B layers l_cut+1..L on one neighbor u_k selected from Pareto Top-K with K=3) overlapped across a mini-batch of M ~ U(2, 32) images with local fallback a_local, operating on a decentralized swarm topology (N=10 default, varied 5..20) with no central coordinator."},{"report_id":"R0118","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_03_accepted.json","key":"partition_assignment","locator":"physical pp. 3-4, §III.A, §III.C","support":"Each UAV hosts one complete specialized MoE expert e_i in {0..M-1} rather than a layer-wise slice of a monolith; a learnable router on the Center UAV (0th UAV) assigns each detected target's whole raw payload to one expert UAV."},{"report_id":"R0118","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0118.pdf","key":"topology_and_planner","locator":"physical pp. 6-8, §V.B-E","support":"Multi-hop FANET relay mesh topology maintained by a centralized online trajectory planner with connectivity tether 0.85 d_comm and fallback, distinct from the per-target expert-selection unit (decentralized MARL alternative also tested)."}],"quantitative":[]}
{"claim_id":"RQ2-A","finding":"In R0006, the optimized decisions are 2D UAV hovering positions G and binary layer-to-UAV allocation X minimizing pipeline completion time T_{I,N} via alternating DP and SCA under memory, energy, exclusive layer assignment, and critical-zone coverage (>= eta) constraints, distinct from its simulation conditions (100m x 100m area, 100 critical zones, fixed altitude H, 5 default UAVs, synthetic 10-24 layer DNN, 100 trials, 3 baselines). In R0064, the optimized decisions are continuous 3D UAV movement, discrete video frame count I, and binary whole-task offloading O (local compressed vs ground ES uncompressed DNN) minimizing average delay via MAPPO-HA under 3D airspace, collision separation D_min, minimum accuracy A_th, PoI coverage U_n(T)>=1, and energy budget E_max constraints, distinct from its simulation conditions (Ubuntu 18.04, 600m x 600m, 3 UAVs, 2 ESs, 50 PoIs, 60 slots, analytical accuracy curve, 2 MHz default uplink, 50 test episodes, 6 baselines).","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0006","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/p08_extraction_table.json","key":"optimization_decisions_and_constraints","locator":"physical p. 4, §III.B, Eq. (15a)-(15f); physical pp. 4-5, §IV","support":"P1 minimizes T_{I,N} over G and X={x_{l,n}} via alternating DP + SCA (CVX/MOSEK) subject to memory (15b), total energy e_{n,cmp}+e_{n,com}+e_{n,hov}<=e_n^{avail} (15c), exclusive binary layer assignment (15d)-(15e), and Coverage>=eta (15f)."},{"report_id":"R0006","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0006.pdf","key":"evaluation_conditions","locator":"physical pp. 6-7, §V.A-C","support":"Evaluation conditions separate from decisions: "we simulated a 100m×100m area containing 100 critical surveillance zones", fixed altitude H, 5 UAVs (5-10 sweep), synthetic 10-24 layer DNN, "averaged over 100 trials with a coverage threshold eta=0.8" (eta=0.7 in Fig. 5), benchmarked against Greedy+SCA, DP+RW, and Joint-PSO."},{"report_id":"R0064","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_01_accepted.json","key":"optimization_decisions_constraints_and_conditions","locator":"physical p. 6, §3.5, Eq. (25a)-(25i); physical pp. 8-9, §5.1-5.2, Table 2","support":"P1 minimizes average inference delay over 3D trajectories Q, sampled frame counts I, and binary whole-task offloading O via MAPPO-HA under airspace/collision (25b), accuracy >= A_th (25f), all-PoI sensing U_n(T)>=1 (25h), and energy <= E_max (25i) constraints; evaluated in Ubuntu 18.04 simulation (600m x 600m, M=3 UAVs, K=2 ESs, N=50 PoIs, T=60 slots, 1080x1080 fixed resolution, analytical accuracy curve with A_th=0.9 and 5% compression loss, B_k=2 MHz default, 50 test episodes, 6 baselines)."}],"quantitative":[]}
{"claim_id":"RQ2-B","finding":"R0058 performs binary whole-request task-to-server assignment (at most one ground edge server per ResNet-18/CIFAR-10 task, with local/buffered fallback and no layer split) targeting a latency-accuracy trade-off under connection-time and server-capacity constraints operationalized by a distributed auction maximizing aggregate buyer-seller utility, whereas R0080 uploads all UAV-extracted features to the cloud and uses per-UAV confidence thresholds to trigger additional quantized raw-data uploads, optimizing thresholds and quantization bit depths via alternating optimization to maximize average classification accuracy under a shared bandwidth limit.","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0058","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_01_accepted.json","key":"offloading_unit_and_objective","locator":"physical p. 5, §3.1; physical p. 8, §3.4, Eq. (10); physical p. 11, §4.2, Eq. (15)","support":"Binary whole-request offloading a_{nm}(t) in {0,1} with sum_m a_{nm}(t)<=1 (no layer split; buffered/local fallback on p. 10 §4.1); Problem P minimizes weighted latency minus accuracy alphad_{nm}^{total}(t) - gammaLambda_{nm}(t) under connection duration (C1) and server capacity (C2), solved by a distributed auction maximizing aggregate utility sum U_n(t) + sum U_m(t)."},{"report_id":"R0080","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_02_accepted.json","key":"confidence_triggered_handling","locator":"physical pp. 2-3, §II.A, Fig. 1","support":"Each aerial UAV extracts features onboard and uploads all features to the cloud; the cloud computes Softmax confidence c_{i,j}, where "c_{i,j}>theta_i indicates that only features are uploaded; otherwise, raw data are transmitted to the cloud" encoded at quantized bit depth b_{k_i}."},{"report_id":"R0080","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0080.pdf","key":"optimization_target","locator":"physical pp. 2-5, §II.B-III, Eq. (11a)-(11f), Algorithm 1","support":""The optimization objective of the system is to design {theta_i} and {b_{k_i}} for all UAVs to maximize A" under shared bandwidth constraint sum R_i/S_i <= B_max via alternating optimization (latency is evaluated descriptively in Table I, not in the objective)."}],"quantitative":[]}
{"claim_id":"RQ3-A","finding":"R0014 separates an indoor benchtop cluster of 24 Jetson airborne computers over Wi-Fi from an outdoor deployment of 5 real-world UAVs using ad-hoc modules, qualified by P09 D07 (PARTIAL) because flight mobility trajectories and wireless channel conditions are uncharacterized. R0137 separates physical hardware profiling (Tables II-IV on 3 DJI Manifold UAVs and an RTX 4080 EC) and reported deployment of offline-trained MARL weights onto the physical testbed from modeled probabilistic LoS/NLoS communications and Poisson failure rates during offline training, qualified by P09 D07 (PARTIAL) because Sim2Real factors like OS scheduling jitter are unmodeled and delay-threshold sweeps are not dedicated fault-injection validation.","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0014","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0014.pdf","key":"indoor_vs_outdoor_testbeds","locator":"physical pp. 10-11, §VIII.A, Fig. 12-13, Table III","support":"Indoor testbed uses "24 UAV airborne computers, including 20 Jetson Nano, 2 Jetson Xavier TX2, and 2 Jetson Xavier NX" over "a WiFi network" (20 Nanos for Table III 720x1280; 11 heterogeneous nodes for Fig. 13(a) 540x960), whereas outdoor testbed uses "5 real-world UAVs, which are equipped with 4 Jetson Nano and 1 Jetson NX, and the UAVs transmit data through the Ad hoc modules on UAVs" (Fig. 13(b) 180x320)."},{"report_id":"R0014","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/appraisal/active_appraisal_rationale_matrix.csv","key":"D07","locator":"physical pp. 10-11, §VIII.A (P09 D07 = PARTIAL)","support":""Evaluates indoor Wi-Fi and five ad-hoc UAVs, without characterizing flight mobility or channels. UAV flight mobility trajectories and wireless channel conditions are uncharacterized.""},{"report_id":"R0137","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0137.pdf","key":"profiling_deployment_vs_modeled_comms","locator":"physical pp. 5-6, §III.B, §III.D; physical pp. 10-11, §VI.A, Tables II-IV","support":""training and physical deployment were conducted in two distinct phases" with Tables II-IV directly profiled on 3 DJI Manifold UAVs and an RTX 4080 EC and trained policy weights deployed to the physical testbed, whereas offline training uses a "probabilistic path-loss model" (3D distance, elevation angles, LoS/NLoS, fast fading) and Poisson computation/transmission failure rates ([0, 0.1] and [0, 0.03])."},{"report_id":"R0137","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/appraisal/supplemental_batch_03_accepted.json","key":"D07","locator":"physical p. 10, §VI.A; physical p. 12, §VI.B (P09 D07 = PARTIAL)","support":"P09 D07 = PARTIAL: "Other Sim2Real factors (e.g. OS scheduling jitter) are not fully modeled in our current simulation, which constitutes a known limitation of this work." (p. 10 §VI.A) and "Note that these comparative experiments under varied permissible delay thresholds do not serve as dedicated fault-injection validation." (p. 12 §VI.B)."}],"quantitative":[]}
{"claim_id":"RQ3-B","finding":"Both R0118 and R0125 receive P09 D04 = PARTIAL for comparability limits. In R0118's common-planner experiment, NetMoE and the layer-wise baseline share the same centralized trajectory planner and ~9.7 MB per-UAV memory footprint, but compare MobileNetV3-Small experts (~2.54M params, ~0.11 GFLOPs/inference) against a monolithic ResNet-50 (~25.56M params, ~8.18 GFLOPs/inference, ~72x higher per-task FLOPs). In R0125's workstation scaling, each added worker receives 4 threads (scaling aggregate compute from 4 threads at 1 worker to 16 threads at 4 workers on an AMD Ryzen 9 7900X) with no external distributed baselines compared.","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0118","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/appraisal/supplemental_batch_03_accepted.json","key":"D04","locator":"physical p. 9, §VI.B; physical p. 11, Table IV (P09 D04 = PARTIAL)","support":"P09 D04 = PARTIAL: "NetMoE and layer-wise baselines share the same centralized trajectory planner and per-UAV memory, but differ in model complexity." Gap: "The layer-wise baseline uses a much larger monolithic DNN than individual MoE experts.""},{"report_id":"R0118","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0118.pdf","key":"model_complexity_difference","locator":"physical p. 9, §VI.B; physical p. 11, Table IV","support":"Common-planner comparison pairs MobileNetV3-Small MoE experts (~2.54M params, ~0.11 GFLOPs/inference, 9.70 MB fp32 per UAV) against a layer-wise partitioned ResNet-50 monolith (~25.56M params, ~8.18 GFLOPs/inference, 9.75 MB fp32 per UAV; ~72x higher per-task FLOPs)."},{"report_id":"R0125","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/appraisal/supplemental_batch_03_accepted.json","key":"D04","locator":"physical p. 3, §IV.A-B, Table I, Fig. 1 (P09 D04 = PARTIAL)","support":"P09 D04 = PARTIAL: "Multi-worker scaling allocates four threads per worker, increasing aggregate compute relative to the single-worker baseline without external distributed baselines.""},{"report_id":"R0125","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0125.pdf","key":"workstation_scaling_capacity","locator":"physical p. 3, §III.D, §IV.A-B, Table I, Fig. 1","support":"On an AMD Ryzen 9 7900X (24 logical cores), "Figure 1 presents throughput scaling with increasing worker count, where each worker is allocated 4 threads" (1 worker/4 threads: 145 ms, 6.9 FPS vs 4 workers/16 threads: 44 ms, 22.4 FPS, 68% latency drop; Table I 16-thread single worker: 78 ms, 12.8 FPS)."}],"quantitative":[]}
{"claim_id":"RQ4-A","finding":"R0002 simulates centralized GCS-guided cross-swarm UAV migration and intra-swarm predictive/recovery service migration for battery replacement (B<=B_min) and random UAV failures (p=0.0001/slot) across 3 swarms (5, 10, 15 UAVs in a 100m triangle, 16-32 Mbps, 12-36 TOPS, 20/40/80 tasks/swarm/slot over 1000 slots, 2-module ResNet-18/CIFAR-10). By contrast, R0092 simulates a decentralized swarm (N=10 default, v_i~U(5,20) m/s, R_comm=200m, ~41% neighbor-change probability/epoch, 5 GHz 802.11ac with 6 dB shadowing, Doppler ICI, 9-level MCS, CSMA, 30 common-seed episodes) handling mid-execution link interruption via a deterministic migrate -> fall back locally -> fail recovery state machine.","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0002","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/p08_extraction_table.json","key":"failure_migration_and_conditions","locator":"physical pp. 3-4, §III.B-C; physical pp. 7-8, §VII.A-B, Fig. 2-5","support":"Centralized GCS runs LST-GNN rolling prediction + PDBH Hungarian migration for battery replacement (B<=B_min, downtime 2*T^{ret}+T^{swap}) and random UAV failures (predictive pre-migration or reactive recovery migration of 2-module ResNet-18/CIFAR-10 tasks)."},{"report_id":"R0002","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0002.pdf","key":"exact_simulated_conditions","locator":"physical p. 7, §VII.A","support":""three swarms are arranged in an equilateral triangular topology with a side length of 100 m, where the numbers of UAVs in the three swarms are set to 5, 10, and 15, respectively", bandwidth [16, 32] Mbps, compute [12, 36] TOPS, "20, 40, or 80 tasks per time-slot" over 1000 slots, and "random UAV failures are introduced in each time-slot with a probability of 0.0001.""},{"report_id":"R0092","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_02_accepted.json","key":"link_interruption_recovery_and_conditions","locator":"physical pp. 3-4, §III.A-C; physical p. 11, Table IV; physical p. 12, §V.F; physical p. 14, Table VII","support":"Decentralized swarm (N=10 default, v_i~U(5,20) m/s, R_comm=200 m, ~41% neighbor-change probability per epoch, 5 GHz 802.11ac, sigma=6 dB shadowing, Doppler ICI, 9-level MCS, CSMA, 30 common-seed episodes) handles mid-execution link loss via migrate (nearest in-range neighbor) -> fall back locally (if remaining deadline > F_B(l_cut)/C_i) -> fail (disabling recovery shifts VGG-16 D.Sat. 71.6 ± 3.4% -> 71.3 ± 4.0%)."}],"quantitative":[]}
{"claim_id":"RQ4-B","finding":"R0038 tests unplanned software/node disconnection and automated container restart on a stationary 4-node benchtop Docker Swarm + Redis testbed (Ubuntu desktop, laptop, 2x Raspberry Pi 5), where disconnecting a Pi running conv_layer1 at t=30 s pauses downstream stages while input ingestion continues until Docker Swarm redeploys the container in ~40 s from Redis checkpoints (+~40% runtime for 100 images, no UAV flight). R0292 instead targets planned low-battery tracking handoff between two Jetson Nano quadrotors (ArUco + ORB matching), evidenced separately by offline tracker evaluation on recorded aerial shark footage, template-padding matching (82.98% cover at 70-px padding), and 180 s hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) onboard tests (4-5 Hz visual / 100+ Hz ArduPilot, 7.4 W avg) without a live ocean flight handover.","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0038","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_01_accepted.json","key":"tested_node_disconnection_restart","locator":"physical pp. 5-6, §V.A-B.2, Table II","support":"Stationary benchtop testbed (Ubuntu desktop, Ubuntu laptop, 2x Raspberry Pi 5; 100 images at 255x255 px): "a device was intentionally removed from the swarm 30 seconds after initialization" (Pi running conv_layer1); "Docker Swarm successfully detected the service interruption and redeployed conv_layer1 to an available node within approximately 40 seconds" resuming from Redis checkpoints with "no data was lost" and "approximately 40% longer to process one hundred images"."},{"report_id":"R0292","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_03_accepted.json","key":"planned_handoff_and_recorded_footage","locator":"physical pp. 3-5, §3.2, §4.1, §5.1-5.2, Tables 1-3, Fig. 3","support":"Planned low-battery handoff between D1 and D2 (Jetson Nano 8 GB, ROS/MAVROS, Wi-Fi, ArUco marker + ORB matching); five trackers benchmarked offline on recorded coastal shark footage (5,200 frames in §5.1 Tables 1-2; 6,928 frames in §3.2) and two-stage template matching evaluated in Table 3 (82.98% target cover, 236-frame longest streak at 70-px padding)."},{"report_id":"R0292","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0292.pdf","key":"hil_onboard_evidence","locator":"physical pp. 5-6, §5.2, Fig. 5","support":""The onboard system tests were performed in a hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) environment, which included visual tracking, feature matching, inter-UAV coordination, and ROS-based navigation logic, resulting in stable end-to-end performance at 4-5 Hz" (ArduPilot 100+ Hz; 180 s run averaging 7.4 W, peak 8.9 W; no live ocean two-UAV handover flight test)."}],"quantitative":[]}
{"claim_id":"RQ5-A","finding":"In R0080's simulated 100 Mbps Table I comparison on synthesized non-identical CIFAR-10 inputs with ResNet-18 (edge) and ResNet-101 (cloud), the proposed scheme reports paired descriptive estimates of 90.4 ± 0.5% top-1 accuracy (vs GSUP: 89.5 ± 0.5%) and 120 ± 3 ms simulated end-to-end inference time per sample (vs GSUP: 117 ± 3 ms), expressed as means with 95% Student-t confidence intervals across three independent seeds, with no statistical separation inference and no latency improvement claimed against GSUP.","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0080","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_02_accepted.json","key":"quantitative_rows","locator":"physical p. 5, Table I, ResNet-18/ResNet-101 block, Ours and GSUP","support":"Verbatim paired P08 rows: top-1 accuracy 90.4 ± 0.5 % (baseline GSUP: 89.5 ± 0.5%) and simulated end-to-end inference time per sample 120 ± 3 ms (baseline GSUP: 117 ± 3 ms), both under 100 Mbps simulated edge-cloud setup with descriptive estimates and no statistical separation claim."},{"report_id":"R0080","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0080.pdf","key":"uncertainty_and_conditions","locator":"physical p. 5, §IV.A, Table I; physical p. 6, §IV.B","support":""Each experimental setting was repeated three times with independent seeds. The reported results are presented as the mean with 95% Student-t confidence intervals. Latency L reflects the simulated end-to-end inference time per sample, encompassing edge processing, data transfer and cloud inference." (p. 5); "Table I provide the accuracy and latency of four different model configurations under a bandwidth of 100 Mbps." (p. 6)."}],"quantitative":[{"metric":"top-1 accuracy","value":"90.4 ± 0.5","units":"%","baseline":"GSUP: 89.5 ± 0.5%","denominator":"mean with 95% Student-t confidence intervals across three independent seeds for each setting","workload":"synthesized non-identical CIFAR-10 test inputs, ResNet-18 edge and ResNet-101 cloud","conditions":"simulated edge–cloud setup; 100 Mbps Table I comparison; descriptive estimates without statistical separation claim","page":5,"locator":"Table I, ResNet-18/ResNet-101 block, Ours and GSUP, Acc. (%)"},{"metric":"simulated end-to-end inference time per sample","value":"120 ± 3","units":"ms","baseline":"GSUP: 117 ± 3 ms","denominator":"mean with 95% Student-t confidence intervals across three independent seeds for each setting","workload":"synthesized non-identical CIFAR-10 test inputs, ResNet-18 edge and ResNet-101 cloud","conditions":"same configuration as paired top-1 result, with edge, transfer and cloud time included; no latency improvement claimed against GSUP","page":5,"locator":"Table I, ResNet-18/ResNet-101 block, Ours and GSUP, L (ms)"}]}
{"claim_id":"RQ5-B","finding":"In R0092's custom Gymnasium simulator on VGG-16 using the strict all-arrived-tasks denominator (mean ± SD over 30 common-seed episodes on a shared two-UAV mini-batch substrate), DDCI (Ours) reports 71.6 ± 3.4% deadline satisfaction against Comm-Min (accepted P08 row records 64.4 ± 3.6%, whereas original PDF Table V physical p. 13 prints 64.4 ± 4.3%—flagged in unresolved). P09 appraisal bounds this evidence to the evaluated simulator, four DNNs, flow-level CSMA model, single-operator swarm assumption (D08 = YES, p. 9 §IV), and excluded propulsion power (D10 = PARTIAL, p. 5 §III.F).","evidence":[{"report_id":"R0092","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_02_accepted.json","key":"quantitative_rows","locator":"physical p. 13, Table V, VGG-16, DDCI (Ours) and Comm-Min rows, Deadline Sat. (%)","support":"Accepted P08 row: deadline satisfaction 71.6 ± 3.4 %, baseline Comm-Min: 64.4 ± 3.6%, denominator strict all-arrived-tasks; mean ± SD over 30 common-seed episodes, workload VGG-16 in the custom Gymnasium simulator, shared two-UAV mini-batch substrate."},{"report_id":"R0092","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0092.pdf","key":"table_v_pdf_verification","locator":"physical p. 13, Table V, VGG-16 block","support":"Original PDF Table V (VGG-16) prints DDCI (Ours) Deadline Sat. (%) = 71.6 ± 3.4 and Comm-Min [3] Deadline Sat. (%) = 64.4 ± 4.3 (note: 3.6 SD appears in GoogLeNet-22 Comm-Min 28.2 ± 3.6, indicating a baseline SD transcription mismatch in the accepted P08 row)."},{"report_id":"R0092","source_path":"restart_2026_11_26/appraisal/supplemental_batch_02_accepted.json","key":"D08_and_D10","locator":"physical p. 9, §IV (P09 D08 = YES); physical p. 5, §III.F (P09 D10 = PARTIAL)","support":"D08 = YES bounds conclusions to the custom Gymnasium simulator, four evaluated DNNs, flow-level CSMA, and "we assume a single-operator swarm" (p. 9 §IV); D10 = PARTIAL notes inline technical boundaries including excluding UAV propulsion power (p. 5 §III.F) and absent COI statement."}],"quantitative":[{"metric":"deadline satisfaction","value":"71.6 ± 3.4","units":"%","baseline":"Comm-Min: 64.4 ± 3.6%","denominator":"strict all-arrived-tasks; mean ± SD over 30 common-seed episodes","workload":"VGG-16 in the custom Gymnasium simulator","conditions":"shared two-UAV mini-batch substrate, different decision policies; result applies to this workload and comparator","page":13,"locator":"Table V, VGG-16, DDCI (Ours) and Comm-Min rows, Deadline Sat. (%)"}]}
{"id":"SUMMARY","claim_count":10,"tools_used":["client:view_file (approved client file viewer only; local PyMuPDF/Poppler/MinerU/Marker unavailable under no-command boundary)","client:grep_search"],"workers_used":false,"limitations":{"RQ1":"Architectural and partitioning contrasts are bounded strictly to the four named reports (R0001, R0014, R0092, R0118) without corpus-wide frequencies or predominance claims; R0081, R0082, and R0132 remain awaiting classification and R0116 remains unretrieved.","RQ2":"Formulated decision variables, constraints, and evaluation setups are bounded to R0006, R0064, R0058, and R0080, all evaluated in simulation with non-interchangeable objectives, channel models, and workloads (synthetic layers in R0006, analytical accuracy curve in R0064, simulated CIFAR-10 in R0058 and R0080).","RQ3":"Testbed realism and baseline comparability in R0014, R0137, R0118, and R0125 are qualified by P09 D07 and D04 PARTIAL judgments, reflecting uncharacterized flight mobility/channels (R0014), unmodeled Sim2Real jitter and absent dedicated fault injection (R0137), asymmetric model complexity (R0118), and expanding aggregate thread capacity without external baselines (R0125).","RQ4":"Resilience evidence across R0002, R0092, R0038, and R0292 is non-equivalent across failure modes and fidelity: R0002 and R0092 are purely simulated, R0038 tests stationary benchtop container restarts without UAV flight, and R0292 evaluates planned low-battery handoff via recorded footage and HIL rather than live open-ocean flight handover; PDF verification used only the approved client file viewer (no local PyMuPDF/Poppler/MinerU/Marker).","RQ5":"Quantitative rows are bounded to single-report descriptive comparisons (R0080 at 3 seeds with 95% Student-t CIs and no statistical separation claim; R0092 on VGG-16 over 30 simulator episodes bounded by P09 D08/D10), with no cross-study pooling and one flagged baseline SD transcription mismatch in R0092 Table V."},"unresolved":[{"item_key":"RQ5-B_R0092_comm_min_sd_mismatch","status":"DISCREPANCY_FLAGGED_VERBATIM_P08_RETAINED","reason":"The accepted P08 quantitative row for R0092 records baseline as 'Comm-Min: 64.4 ± 3.6%', whereas the original PDF R0092.pdf (physical p. 13, Table V, VGG-16 block) prints '64.4 ± 4.3' for Comm-Min [3] Deadline Sat. (%) (the 3.6 SD belongs to GoogLeNet-22 Comm-Min 28.2 ± 3.6). The accepted P08 row was copied verbatim into RQ5-B.quantitative per instructions and this discrepancy is flagged for Codex adjudication.","sources_checked":["restart_2026_11_26/extraction/supplemental_batch_02_accepted.json","restart_2026_11_26/fulltext_retrieval/pdf/R0092.pdf"]}]}
